                          HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Is that why I hear things?” I said. “Things other people don’t?”
   “It’s why you need noise to think, and why a quiet room feels like a
hand on your chest.” She didn’t blink. “If you’re hearing things that
aren’t there, that’s a different appointment, and I’d want to make it.”
   “They’re there.”
   “Then bring me a recording,” said Dr. Okafor. “I’ll listen to anything
once.”
   I looked at the fish. The fish looked at me.
   “Can I ask you something, hypothetically?”
   “Everyone does.”
   “If you put a kid like me in a room with all the noise taken out of it,
and played one low, even tone. What would that do?”
   She didn’t answer right away.
   “Hypothetically,” she said, “it would be a very bad classroom. And
someone would write down his behavior.”
   “Somebody did.”
   She held my eyes a second longer than a doctor strictly has to.
   “I’ll see you next Thursday,” Dr. Okafor said. “Bring someone with a
spreadsheet.”



I told my mother at dinner.
    I’d rehearsed it all day, in my head, on the train, in a stall at a bakery.
I had a speech. It was careful, and it began Má, I don’t want you to think
I’m making an excuse, because some old reflex was sure she’d hear it as
vago with a doctor’s note.
    I got as far as “Má—”
    “You went to a doctor,” she said, and put her spoon down.
    “How—”
    “You ate breakfast without me asking. Twice.” She looked at me
with the total inspection, steadily. “Dime.”
    I said the word. I said it plain, because I didn’t have a better way.
